                    <div class="col-lg-6 d-flex justify-content-center align-items-center pl-lg-4">
                        <div class="testimonial-content-box bg_cover wow fadeInRight justify-content-center align-items-start  d-flex flex-column" style="background-image: none !important; visibility: visible; animation-name: fadeInRight;">
                            <div class="section-title section-title-left pb-0 ">
                                <span class="sub-title pt-4" style="color: white !important;text-align: start !important;font-size: 25px !important;">
                                    Dry Food</span>
                            </div>
                            <p style="color: white !important;">When it comes to feeding our feline friends, the choice between dry and wet food can be a puzzling one. Dry food, often seen as a convenient and economical option, has its fair share of supporters and detractors. Understanding the benefits and drawbacks of dry food can help cat owners make informed decisions about their pet's nutrition.</p>
                            <div class="section-title section-title-left pb-0 ">
                                <span class="sub-title pt-4" style="color: white !important;text-align: start !important;font-size: 25px !important;">
                                    Pros of Dry Food for Cats:</span>
                            </div>
                           
                            <p style="color: white !important;display: flex;padding-bottom: 15px;"><i class="bi bi-arrow-bar-right mr-2"></i> Convenience: One of the most significant advantages of dry cat food is its convenience. It can be left out for cats to graze on throughout the day without the risk of spoilage, making it a favored choice for pet owners with busy schedules.</p style="color: white !important;">
                            <p style="color: white !important;display: flex;padding-bottom: 15px;"><i class="bi bi-arrow-bar-right mr-2"></i> Cost-Effectiveness: Generally, dry food is more economical than wet food. It comes in larger packages that last longer, offering more meals per dollar spent.</p style="color: white !important;">
                            <p style="color: white !important;display: flex;padding-bottom: 15px;"><i class="bi bi-arrow-bar-right mr-2"></i> Dental Health: Some veterinarians argue that the crunchy texture of dry food can help reduce tartar build-up on a cat's teeth, potentially contributing to better overall dental health.</p style="color: white !important;">
                                <p style="color: white !important;display: flex;padding-bottom: 15px;"><i class="bi bi-arrow-bar-right mr-2"></i>Less Messy: Dry food is less messy and easier to clean up than wet food, which can be a significant advantage in maintaining a clean feeding area.</p style="color: white !important;">
                        </div>
                        
                    </div>
                    
                </div>

            </div>
        </div>
        
    </section>

    <section class="testimonial-area pb-0    pt-0">
        <div class="container py-3">
            <div class="testimonial-wrapper-two">
                <div class="row no-gutters" style=" visibility: visible; animation-name: fadeInRight;">
                   
                    <div class="col-lg-6 order-1-class  justify-content-center align-items-center pr-lg-4">
                        <div class="testimonial-content-box bg_cover wow fadeInRight justify-content-center align-items-start  d-flex flex-column" style="background-image: none !important; visibility: visible; animation-name: fadeInRight;">
                            <div class="section-title section-title-left pb-0 ">
                                <span class="sub-title pt-4" style="color: black !important;text-align: start !important;font-size: 25px !important;">
                                    Cons of Dry Food for Cats:</span>
                            </div>
                           
                           
                            <p style="color: black !important;display: flex;padding-bottom: 15px;"><i class="bi bi-arrow-bar-right mr-2"></i> Moisture Content: The most critical issue with dry food is its low moisture content. Cats naturally have a low thirst drive and derive much of their water intake from their food. A diet exclusively based on dry food can lead to dehydration and is often linked to urinary tract diseases.</p style="color: black !important;">
                            <p style="color: black !important;display: flex;padding-bottom: 15px;"><i class="bi bi-arrow-bar-right mr-2"></i> Carbohydrate Levels: Dry foods typically contain higher levels of carbohydrates than wet foods. High carbohydrate intake can lead to obesity and diabetes, conditions increasingly seen in domestic cats.</p style="color: black !important;">
                            <p style="color: black !important;display: flex;padding-bottom: 15px;"><i class="bi bi-arrow-bar-right mr-2"></i> Variability in Quality: The quality of dry food can vary significantly between brands. Some may use high levels of fillers and low-quality ingredients that offer less nutritional value for your cat.</p style="color: black !important;">
                                <p style="color: black !important;display: flex;padding-bottom: 15px;"><i class="bi bi-arrow-bar-right mr-2"></i>Palatability: While many cats enjoy dry food, some may find it less palatable than wet food. This can especially be a problem for picky eaters or cats with certain health issues that affect their appetite.</p style="color: black !important;">
